## The work — a checklist over the new folder

### ➕ Add — `boukensha/run_dsl.py`

The whole class, mirroring Ruby's 13 lines:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `RunDSL#initialize(registry)` | `def __init__(self, registry)` |
| `tool(name, description:, parameters: {}, &block)` | `def tool(self, name, *, description, parameters=None)` returning a decorator |
| delegates to `@registry.tool(...)` | `return self.registry.tool(name, description=description, parameters=parameters)` |

`Registry.tool` already returns a decorator (`register`), so `RunDSL.tool` is a
one-line passthrough and `@dsl.tool(...)` works exactly like `@registry.tool(...)`.
Keep the surface to `tool` alone — Ruby's comment is explicit that the small
surface is the point.

### ➕ Add — `boukensha/run.py`

`Boukensha.run` ported as a module-level `run(...)`. See **OQ 2** for why it is
its own module rather than `__init__.py`.

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `def self.run(task:, system: nil, model: nil, backend: nil, api_key: nil, ollama_host: "http://localhost:11434", log: nil, max_output_tokens: nil, &block)` | `def run(*, task, system=None, model=None, backend=None, api_key=None, ollama_host="http://localhost:11434", log=None, max_output_tokens=None, tools=None)` |
| `cfg = config` | `cfg = runtime.config()` — memoized, and the call that loads `.env` into the environment |
| `task_class = Tasks::Player` | `task_class = Player` |
| `cfg.tasks(task_class.task_name)` | `cfg.tasks(task_class.task_name())` — **call it**, same trap as step 06's logger |
| `backend ||= task_class.provider(task_settings).to_sym` | keep it a **string**; Python has no symbols and every existing comparison is string-based |
| `api_key ||= case backend …` | a dict lookup `{"anthropic": "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY", …}.get(backend)` into `os.environ.get(...)`; `ollama` is absent from the table and stays `None` |
| `RunDSL.new(registry).instance_eval(&block) if block` | `tools(RunDSL(registry))` if `tools` — see **OQ 1** |
| `case backend … else raise ArgumentError` | if/elif chain ending in `raise ValueError`, message wording matched |
| `Ollama.new(host: ollama_host, model: model)` | `backends.Ollama(host=ollama_host, model=model)` — Python's `Ollama.__init__` already takes `host` |
| `logger&.close` in `ensure` | `try/finally` with `logger = None` initialized **before** the `try` — Ruby defines the local at parse time, Python raises `UnboundLocalError` |
| `ctx.add_message(:user, task)` then `agent.run` | `ctx.add_message("user", task)` then `return agent.run()` |

The `logger` snapshot is new and matters — it is what `log_viz` reads for its
denominators:

```python
snapshot = {
    "task": task_class.task_name(),
    "max_iterations": effective_max_iterations,
    "max_output_tokens": effective_max_output_tokens,
    "model": model,
    "provider": backend,
}
```

Key order must match Ruby's. Note what is **absent**: `max_turn_tokens` and
`context_window`. `log_viz` will therefore render the iterations bar for
step-07 logs but still skip the peak-context and largest-turn bars — expected,
not a regression. (Those keys arrive with the `.run` DSL in step 12.)

> ⚠️ **`max_output_tokens` is resolved once and used twice.** Ruby computes
> `effective_max_output_tokens = max_output_tokens || task_class.max_output_tokens(task_settings)`
> and passes it to *both* the logger snapshot and `Agent`. Ruby's `||` here is a
> truthiness test, so an explicit `max_output_tokens: 0` would fall through to
> the task default — Python's `if max_output_tokens is None` would not. **This
> one is the reverse of the usual trap:** the faithful port of Ruby's behaviour
> is `or`, not `is None`. Recommend matching Ruby with `or` and noting it,
> because `Agent._call_opts` already guards `0` correctly one layer down and
> diverging here would make the two languages disagree. Flag it in the README.

### ✏️ Edit — `boukensha/logger.py`

Add two methods, in Ruby's positions:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `turn(n:)` — before `iteration` | `def turn(self, *, n)` writing `{"phase": "turn", "n": n}` |
| `subscribe(&block)` — after `raw`, before `close` | `def subscribe(self, callback)` appending to a lazily-created list |
| `@subscribers&.each { \|s\| s.call(event) }` at the end of `write_log` | same, **after** the write and flush — subscribers must not be able to prevent the line landing on disk |

Ruby lazily initializes `@subscribers ||= []` inside `subscribe`; Python should
initialize `self._subscribers = []` in `__init__` instead. Ruby's lazy form
exists because an uninitialized ivar is `nil` rather than an error; Python has
no such excuse, and `write_log` already has to handle the empty case.

